[[0,537023,0,2,0,2,"2015-09-24T05:41:48","muffinmandible","",[],"","Trailer - The Angry Birds Movie","https://youtu.be/0qJzWrq7les","movies"],[1,2562258,0,2,0,2,"2015-09-24T05:42:39","muffinmandible","",[],"<p>It looks surprising good. The trailer actually made me chuckle.</p>"],[1,2563359,0,1,0,1,"2015-09-24T07:52:17","torky","[e]",[],"<p>I don't like where the movie originated from (the Angry Birds mobile game), but so far, the trailer doesn't make it seem so bad. I'm actually going to hold my judgement until I see more clips or actually watch the movie. I hope the movie surprises me, it might not be bad at all.</p>\n<p>Of course, the last film that was based on videogames was a flop. However, this time, Hollywood is cashing in on something trendy and not on nostalgia from the 80's. </p>\n<p>I hope it goes 'Wreck-It-Ralph' instead of 'Pixels'</p>"]]
[[0,537024,0,5,0,5,"2015-09-24T05:41:57","someguyfromcanada","",[],"","Terry Fox runs in his blood stained shorts, during his Marathon of Hope run across Canada. He ran a marathon a day for 143 days. Made it half way home until he succumbed to cancer. 1980.","http://sli.mg/images/2015/09/20/f0c148bbb8c806101f44bd9a372f1ddc.jpg","Running"],[1,2562477,0,3,0,3,"2015-09-24T06:05:26","someguyfromcanada","[e]",[],"<p>A life-long athlete, Terry Fox had a leg amputated due to bone cancer. The night before his surgery, Fox was given an article about Dick Traum, the first amputee to complete the New York City Marathon. The article inspired him and he subsequently embarked on a 14-month training program. </p>\n<p>He then envisioned and began the Marathon of Hope, a cross-Canada run to raise money for cancer research on April 12, 1980, when he dipped his right leg into the Atlantic Ocean near St. John's, Newfoundland, and filled two large bottles with ocean water. He intended to keep one as a souvenir and pour the other into the Pacific Ocean upon completing his journey at Victoria, British Columbia. He hoped to raise one dollar from each of Canada's 24 million people. He began with little fanfare from the eastern point and ran a full marathon every day. </p>\n<p>Although initially disappointed by the media awareness and the lack of support from the Canadian Cancer Society, he was heartened upon arriving in Port aux Basques, Newfoundland where the town's 10,000 residents presented him with a donation of over $10,000. Fox had become a national star by the time he reached Toronto, which was not even the mid-point of Canada. </p>\n<p>This picture was taken in a suburb of Toronto. He never made it out of that province on his own two legs. On September 1, outside of Thunder Bay, he was forced to stop with shortness of breath, continued physical and chest pain and went to a hospital. </p>\n<p>The next day, Fox held a tearful press conference during which he announced that his cancer had returned and spread to his lungs. He was forced to end his run after 143 days and 5,373 kilometres (3,339 mi). He vowed to complete the run to raise more awareness and money. He had raised $1.7 million by that time but would never run again.</p>\n<p>Since then the Terry Fox Run has been an annual non-competitive commemorative charity event held in over 40 countries to raise awareness and money for cancer research. It has raised over $650 million despite the fact that Terry insisted upon there never being any corporate sponsorship or advertising on any shirts or banner, etc. so as to mostly raise awareness for the cause. </p>\n<p>Sorry for the lengthy write-up.... but this guy is a fucking hero and deserves it. </p>\n<p><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terry_Fox_Run\">One source among many</a>.</p>"],[2,3721281,2562477,1,0,1,"2015-12-31T06:01:39","WhatDaDuce","",[],"<p>Damn, that hit me right in the feels dude.</p>"]]